WEBVTT

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.593
<v><b>Presenter:</b></v> So now we’re going to talk to Martha,

00:00:02.967 --> 00:00:06.550
who works in the Human Resources department of a large publishing company…

00:00:06.877 --> 00:00:07.564
<v><b>Martha:</b></v> Hello.

00:00:08.109 --> 00:00:08.721
<v><b>Presenter:</b></v> Martha,

00:00:08.988 --> 00:00:14.641
do you think this idea of one hour of work and 20 minutes of rest would work in a big company?

00:00:15.054 --> 00:00:19.789
I mean, if you think about it, in an eight-hour day, and taking lunch into account,

00:00:20.062 --> 00:00:23.403
people would actually only be working for about six hours.

00:00:23.763 --> 00:00:25.083
Do you think that’s feasible?

00:00:25.389 --> 00:00:27.701
<v><b>Martha:</b></v> Well, first I’d say that in fact,

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.888
in an office environment, that is what actually happens a lot of the time.

00:00:32.482 --> 00:00:36.394
I mean, people aren’t tied to their desks for eight hours a day,

00:00:36.648 --> 00:00:39.807
they spend a lot of time having conversations with colleagues.

00:00:40.062 --> 00:00:44.228
I’m not sure that the office environment really allows you to concentrate for a full hour

00:00:44.228 --> 00:00:48.020
without interruption, unless people are really disciplined.

00:00:49.093 --> 00:00:54.398
But, as a concept, I think it’s probably a good idea, and might make people more productive.

00:00:54.885 --> 00:00:58.908
Where I’m not sure that it works like that is for people who are working from home,

00:00:59.074 --> 00:01:02.749
because they may not want to take those 20-minute breaks;

00:01:03.116 --> 00:01:08.836
they may prefer to work non-stop for a few very intensive hours, much fewer than eight,

00:01:09.129 --> 00:01:10.803
and then stop altogether.

00:01:11.331 --> 00:01:14.421
And they can do this because they have fewer distractions

00:01:14.587 --> 00:01:16.924
– they can switch off emails and Skype and things.

00:01:17.591 --> 00:01:22.216
But, actually, they might have achieved the same in three or four intensive hours

00:01:22.289 --> 00:01:24.964
as someone in an office does in an eight-hour day.

00:01:25.258 --> 00:01:29.210
<v><b>Presenter:</b></v> What about the four-day week, something that has also been suggested?

00:01:29.396 --> 00:01:33.466
Can’t this sometimes cause a problem for employers and, indeed, employees?

00:01:33.933 --> 00:01:38.887
<v><b>Martha:</b></v> Both of the large organizations I’ve worked for have been supportive of flexible working,

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:42.681
and quite a few people do work a four-day week now.

00:01:43.261 --> 00:01:50.401
The issue is that some people who do this – who work, say, 32 hours a week very productively,

00:01:50.601 --> 00:01:54.224
instead of the full 40 – they do get paid less.

00:01:54.585 --> 00:01:58.108
But they think that they achieve as much as someone doing five days’ work

00:01:58.294 --> 00:02:03.829
because they work more efficiently, and it’s not fair that they only get paid for four days.

00:02:04.189 --> 00:02:09.861
On the other hand, so the argument goes, they do get the flexibility to have a longer weekend,

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.808
which may suit their lifestyle better.

00:02:12.288 --> 00:02:15.489
But of course, it does depend on the job; obviously,

00:02:15.489 --> 00:02:19.410
if an employer needs staff to be in the office every day for some reason,

00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:21.508
then that’s going to be less feasible.

00:02:21.948 --> 00:02:25.989
I know for employees nearing retirement age, at my company,

00:02:26.095 --> 00:02:30.332
we’re very open to considering that option for them if that’s what they want,

00:02:30.718 --> 00:02:35.094
and I do think that this four-day-week pattern is going to become more common in the future.

00:02:35.521 --> 00:02:38.580
<v><b>Presenter:</b></v> Do you think either of these ideas would work for students,

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:42.306
i.e. that they should take a 15-minute break for every hour of work,

00:02:42.586 --> 00:02:44.628
or only work four days a week?

00:02:44.933 --> 00:02:48.228
<v><b>Martha:</b></v> Well, most university students could easily work that way

00:02:48.281 --> 00:02:51.513
if they wanted to and maybe it would be more productive.

00:02:51.907 --> 00:02:53.977
But somehow, in practice,

00:02:54.151 --> 00:02:58.845
a lot of students I know seem to do very little academic work for a period of time;

00:02:59.032 --> 00:03:02.931
they leave things till the last minute and then suddenly work all night

00:03:02.997 --> 00:03:04.697
when they’ve got an exam coming up!

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:09.520
I’m pretty sure that that’s not the best way or the most productive way to work, though.